The 2008 Financial Crisis was a consequence of intricate, chaotic events affecting various individuals and groups with conflicting financial interests. Audit professionals face a significant challenge in uncovering the motivations behind financial transactions. Our research investigates how to employ Dempster-Shafer mass functions to represent multiple entities' motivations through a framework. A crucial aspect of this framework involves using basic actions an individual can perform to indicate their interests, such as authorizing transactions or holding assets. Through mathematical derivation, it's possible to combine the interests of different entities, as well as quantify the degrees of conflict and consensus among them. Experimental data on Chinese stock market performance from 2010 to 2019 are used to demonstrate the practical applications of our proposed framework. The findings reveal a significant shift in the collective interests of Chinese investors during this time period, as well as varying levels of conflict and consensus. Consistent with real-world scenarios, our approach avoids resolution problems and produces intuitive, meaningful results. Notably, the degrees of interest and conflict measured through the use of our framework correlate positively with actual financial losses and gains, respectively. Not only does this approach hold promise for financial auditing, but it may also be applicable to other fields such as political analysis and big data forecasting. Ultimately, the framework enables a new, pioneering approach to understanding complex social phenomena by investigating the dynamics and relationships between different entities' interests over time. A deeper exploration of this concept is proposed in the conclusion. A related, open-access GitHub project has been established to facilitate easy implementation of the proposed framework in practical applications. The documentation provided explains both the coding and mathematical underpinnings of 